9. Test the complete flow
Register
POST http://localhost:8081/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json


{
    "email": "vini@gmail.com",
    "password": "password123"
}

Expected:

201 Created
Login
POST http://localhost:8081/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json


{
    "email": "vini@gmail.com",
    "password": "password123"
}

Expected:

{
    "accessToken": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...",
    "tokenType": "Bearer"
}

Copy the token.

Call protected API
GET http://localhost:8081/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...

Expected:

Authenticated user: vini@gmail.com

Without the token:

GET /auth/me

you should get:

401 Unauthorized
What we've built so far
                  PayRoute Auth Service
                         │
             ┌───────────┴───────────┐
             │                       │
       POST /register          POST /login
             │                       │
             ▼                       ▼
        BCrypt Hash             Verify Password
             │                       │
             ▼                       ▼
        PostgreSQL                  JWT
                                     │
                                     ▼
                              Access Token
                                     │
                                     ▼
                              Protected APIs
Interview questions this step gives you

Why BCrypt?

Because passwords should not be stored as plaintext; BCrypt is a deliberately slow password hashing algorithm designed to make brute-force attacks more expensive.

Why JWT?

Because the client can carry the authentication information with each request, which works well with stateless services.

Why 401 instead of 403 for missing/invalid authentication?

401 → you're not authenticated.
403 → you're authenticated but don't have permission.

Why Stateless?

Because the authentication state isn't stored as a server-side HTTP session.

